u  know that was pre motion capture andy  serkis would make certain choices and  the animators would keyframe matching in  with that but for the most part what  you'll see if you go to an animation  house is they'll listen to the voices  and I'll have a mirror next to the next  to their monitor and they'll make faces  and you'll get performances that feel  fine and animation but it's very  exaggerated if you saw it in a  live-action film and so I wanted to  eliminate the going the the temptation  to go bigger with their voice or with  their performance and I wanted all the  timing and overlapping that you don't  get when you're doing it alone and we  try to do it with as many actors as we  could we would do pickups later at music  stands or if people like Donald Glover  for some of the sessions was doing solo  in in London so we'd get him and Chu  hotel together in London so we did  everything we could but but under the  best circumstances we would have it  basically look at a theatre performance  we would be in a black box theater  everything would be miked and I would  have them and then I could let them  improv and we could really have a lot of  fun you know there weren't that many  areas with a movie like Lion King where  you could take liberties without  undermining people's expectations of the  title the comedy team won in Pumbaa  things like that where we felt that we  had some some free Running Room we went  crazy with it the takeaway is something  that really puts the technology on a on  a pedestal and shows the state of the  art of what could be  because as you point out it's not the  computers doing it this is the most  handmade movie I've ever worked on and  these tools that they have available  allow the artist to hand paint every  frame every performance every facial  expression every blade of grass every  environment is built it is a  hand-painted tile in a big mosaic and so  what I'm most proud of is how the whole  team came together to present this 